      ["Implement structured non-pharmacologic behavioral and environmental modifications, establish a consistent daily routine, and offer caregiver support resources", "The patient is experiencing behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia (BPSD), specifically 'sundowning' (agitation and confusion worsening in late afternoon/evening). According to American Psychiatric Association (APA) and American Geriatrics Society (AGS) guidelines, NON-PHARMACOLOGIC BEHAVIORAL AND ENVIRONMENTAL INTERVENTIONS ARE THE MANDATORY FIRST-LINE TREATMENT FOR BPSD. Interventions include: (1) establishing predictable daily routines, (2) daytime exposure to sunlight and structured physical activities to reinforce circadian rhythms, (3) minimizing evening environmental overstimulation (noise, clutter, multiple caregivers), (4) validating emotions and gentle redirection rather than confronting disorientation, and (5) caregiver respite and support. PHARMACOLOGIC SAFETY PRINCIPLE: Antipsychotic medications (both typical like haloperidol and atypical like quetiapine, risperidone, olanzapine) carry an FDA BLACK-BOX WARNING for an increased risk of stroke, cardiovascular events, and all-cause mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis. Antipsychotics should be reserved strictly as a last resort for severe, refractory neuropsychiatric symptoms where the patient poses imminent danger to self or others."],
